WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.960
Hey, welcome to SpaceX Daily, Episode 43, it's July 24th, 2026, and I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:13.980
Plenty moving across Starship, Falcon, and Starlink today. Tropical Storm Bertha forces Starship

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:19.200
Flight 13 to Friday, extending the test campaign's weather-driven pauses at Starbase.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:25.680
Remember, the show covered Starship yesterday. Today's delay moves that forward because the vehicle

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:31.060
and Super Heavy Booster remain stacked on the orbital launch mount while teams monitor wind

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:38.160
and lightning constraints. SpaceX postponed the Starship Flight 13 attempt from July 24th after

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:44.800
forecasters tracked Tropical Storm Bertha moving toward the South Texas coast. This marks the second

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.720
weather-related slip in the current campaign window according to Yahoo Tech. The next available

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:56.720
opportunity opens Friday once the storm clears the launch corridor. Starship's flight test

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:02.940
campaign remains focused on booster catch and full reuse goals. And today's delay tests whether

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:08.500
the program can maintain momentum toward orbital refueling demonstrations without resetting hardware

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:15.980
timelines. NASA selected Falcon Heavy to launch the NROL 95 Space Weather Mission after repeated

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:22.080
delays with the Vulcan Centaur vehicle. The switch returns the payload to a proven heavy lift

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:28.440
configuration already cleared for national security flights from Cape Canaveral. Falcon Heavy will

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:34.280
carry the mission's instruments focused on solar wind and geomagnetic monitoring. The decision

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:40.320
underscores ongoing cadence advantages for the Falcon family on time-sensitive national security

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:47.720
manifests per space reporting. Falcon 9 and heavy reuse counts continue climbing and this reassignment

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:53.380
shows how the program's annual launch rate ceiling directly influences government manifest decisions

00:01:53.380 --> 00:02:00.360
when newer vehicles slip. Early reports describe the Gen 3 Starlink design as carrying substantially

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:05.820
larger phased array antennas and higher power solar arrays sized for Starship deployment.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:11.540
The satellites are expected to support expanded direct to cell capacity and improved

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:18.120
inter-satellite laser links according to SDX Central. Production quantities remain tied to Starship

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:24.100
flight rate milestones. The upgrade path directly addresses current constellation throughput limits

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:30.580
reported in prior coverage. Starlink's constellation expansion and direct to cell roll-out continue.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:36.620
And Gen 3 hardware moves the open question of subscriber growth and profitability forward by

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:42.120
increasing per satellite capacity. Once Starship cadence supports higher deployment rates.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:48.520
The article outlines shared infrastructure plans linking XAI training clusters with Starlink

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:55.360
backhaul and potential orbital compute nodes. SpaceX hardware is positioned to supply both terrestrial

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:01.040
power routing and satellite based data movement for the combined stack per MEXC reporting.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:07.640
No specific hardware quantities or timelines are disclosed beyond existing Starlink laser link

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:14.060
demonstrations. The integration path remains conceptual pending further Starship derived capacity.

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:20.600
Traders noted continued pressure on competing launch and spectrum stocks after SpaceX extended

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:27.460
its Falcon 9 manifest lock on certain commercial slots. The freeze limits near-term availability for

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:33.600
rival operators seeking secondary launch capacity according to stocktwits coverage. Retail

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:38.840
commentary centered on the competitive asymmetry created by SpaceX's high launch cadence.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:45.320
The piece details how XAI's model development now draws on engineering telemetry and orbital

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:52.040
data pathways supplied through SpaceX systems. Integration includes potential use of Starlink for

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:58.500
low latency inference routing between ground clusters and future space-based nodes per QZ.com.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:04.740
No dollar figures or hardware allocations appear in the reporting. The arrangement remains at the

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:11.960
planning stage. Elon Musk referenced an upcoming GROC 4.6 model at roughly 2 trillion parameters during

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:17.660
a public exchange comparing capabilities with Chinese competitors. The posts did not include

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:24.380
training timeline or cluster details according to finance.bago coverage. Capability claims remain

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:30.940
unverified pending release. The report explores how orbital compute concepts could increase demand for

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:38.340
high bandwidth, radiation tolerant, GPU architectures supplied by Nvidia. SpaceX's Starlink laser

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:44.320
network is cited as the potential data fabric connecting ground training runs to on-orbit inference

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:51.900
nodes per AOL.com. No contracts or deployment schedules are confirmed. The discussion stays at

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:58.300
the conceptual level. Retail traders on stocktwits discussed whether a successful starship flight 13

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:07.480
could lift SPCX back toward $150, citing recent share weakness tied to test delays. Community posts

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:13.720
highlighted the NRL-95 mission reassignment to Falcon Heavy as evidence of sustained national

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:19.120
security reliance on existing SpaceX vehicles while Vulcan remains grounded.

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:25.960
Forum threads examined the proposed Gen3 Starlink antenna scale-up with amateur analysts comparing

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:32.700
expected beam counts to current V2 mini-designs. Worth keeping an eye on is whether internal timelines

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:38.840
for orbital data centers and lunar infrastructure align with public statements, noting repeated slips

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:45.080
in Starship test cadence. The piece cites the gap between announced milestones and demonstrated hardware

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:52.080
performance according to AOL.com. Resolution would require visible progress on the next two flight

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:59.160
tests and concrete orbital refueling demonstrations. On the AI front, Musk referenced a 2 trillion

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:07.100
parameter GROC 4.6 model in direct comparison to China's Kimi K3, with no training cluster size

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:13.700
disclosed. XAI's integration with SpaceX systems was described as relying on Starlink laser

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:18.900
links for potential low latency routing between ground clusters and future orbital nodes.

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:25.620
Nvidia's position in orbital data center concepts was tied to radiation tolerant GPU demand,

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:31.200
with Starlink positioned as the interconnect fabric. From an engineering standpoint,

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:37.400
launch commit criteria for orbital-class vehicles rest on a narrow set of atmospheric variables that

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:42.880
directly affects structural loads and control authority. Wind shear, lightning probability,

00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:48.900
and upper-level winds each impose hard limits because the vehicle must maintain a stable trajectory

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:53.460
while its engines deliver maximum thrust through the dense lower atmosphere.

00:06:54.420 --> 00:07:00.320
Tropical storm berthas approach forces a multi-day stand-down because even modest increases in

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:05.660
cross-range wind can exceed the gimbal authority margins designed into the booster's flight control

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:11.960
system. The decision to scrub therefore protects both the test article and the ground infrastructure

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:18.480
from an unrecoverable trajectory deviation. Starships current weather tolerance is set by the same

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:24.640
first principles trade that shaped its tank geometry and engine count. Larger propellant loads give

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:30.180
the vehicle enough impulse to reach the required velocity even after a late-day scrub recovery,

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:36.620
yet they also increase the mass that must be steered through gusts. The design therefore accepts a

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:42.580
narrower launch envelope in exchange for simpler tank manufacturing and higher payload fraction once

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:49.120
the weather window opens. This choice keeps the idiot index, the ratio of finished vehicle cost

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:55.100
to raw propellant and metal, lower than a more weather hardened but heavier architecture would allow.

00:07:55.840 --> 00:08:01.980
The raw inputs of stainless steel sheet, liquid oxygen, and methane set a floor cost that reuse

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:07.920
and rapid iteration aimed to approach, but the added mass margin for weather resilience would

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:12.880
raise that floor by increasing both material volume and the energy needed to lift it.

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:19.000
The next engineering observable is how quickly the stacked vehicle can be returned to a launch-ready

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:25.580
state once bertha clears. Pad systems must re-verify propellant conditioning, avionics health,

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:31.720
and tower interfaces after the storm passes. Any extension beyond Friday would indicate whether the

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:37.720
current ground support architecture can sustain the rapid turn cadence required for the orbital refueling

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:43.180
and booster catch milestones still ahead in the campaign. Launch infrastructure readiness

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:48.120
at starbase directly limits how many attempts can fit inside a given weather window.

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:54.040
And today's delay highlights the interaction between external constraints and the pad tower systems

00:08:54.040 --> 00:09:04.140
that set the practical flight rate. And a quick market note, SPCX is at $118.24 up 1.8%.

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:09.380
Before we go, Starship's next clear window will show whether the test cadence can absorb

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:14.780
repeated weather holds without resetting the reuse timeline. And that's a wrap on today's

00:09:14.780 --> 00:09:25.420
SpaceX developments. SPCX is trading at $118.24, up 1.8%. Share this with a fellow space flight fan

